  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/6/62/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_47.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Vital joints of Army tanks are found to have suddenly melted away, and Tony Stark is quickly brought in since his plants <i>built</i> the tanks.&nbsp; Stark can't believe it, since he <i>knows</i> he buys only high-quality parts, but when he arrives to personally inspect the matter, he finds the Melter waiting for him, and soon, he is ambushed.\n\nThe reader is then told that his motive is twofold.&nbsp; First, he wants to \"slow up the Army's defensive effort,\" and second, \"get revenge on my old rival, Tony Stark!\"&nbsp; With Stark unconscious before him, he'll never know that the Melter is actually one of his former competitors, Bruno Horgan.\n\nThe flashback over the next page shows how Horgan's company was discovered using \"inferior materials\" and dropped by the military in favor of Stark's company for the purpose of supplying tanks.&nbsp; Outraged over the loss of the government contract, Horgan nevertheless continued his research and ultimately stumbled across a way of dissolving iron (which itself was an accident brought about by equipment in his own laboratory using inferior parts).\n\nHorgan immediately realized the potential of developing this device and using it to ruin Stark's reputation.&nbsp; He viewed the ability to dissolve... or \"melt\" iron as something that gave him a unique opportunity to \"become the most powerful man in the world!\"&nbsp; After more days of secret work, Horgan finally adapted the device to fit inside a portable unit that could become part of a uniform.&nbsp; Together with a mask to conceal his identity, the Melter was soon born!\n\nReturning to the present, the Melter dissolves the inventory that Stark was inspecting and leaves him lying there, so that he can toy with him some more.&nbsp; He leaves Stark and then sneaks around his munitions factory and ultimately finds his way to its main power plant.&nbsp; Destroying <i>this</i> will allow him to take out the entire factory!\n\nBut in the meantime, Stark has recovered and repowered his chest plate.&nbsp; The Melter's entrance is detected on Stark's alarm board, and soon, Tony dons his armor, and Iron Man races toward the scene!&nbsp; However, by the time he arrives in the power plant, the Melter has already begun destroying the generators, and the moment he spots Iron Man, he turns his destructive beam toward him!\n\nTo his horror, the beam dissolves the armor on his left arm, forcing Iron Man to flee or risk damage to his chest device!&nbsp; He drives the Melter away by forcing hot steam into the area before he can do any more damage.\n\nBut Stark is now faced with major repairs and an inability to face his foe without being melted away and exposing, not only his true identity, but possibly damaging his life-giving chest plate!&nbsp; He leaves orders not to be disturbed as he mulls over how to defeat an enemy that can destroy his armor with one pass of his destructive beam.\n\nWhile he is out of sight, Happy and Pepper keep flirting as they deal with his absence by turning away visiting Hollywood stars.&nbsp; And for its part, the US government is threatening to cancel all their military contracts if Stark doesn't solve the melting problems inside his tanks (and they don't even believe him when he says an actual villain, the Melter, is responsible).\n\nStark is soon summoned back to Long Island and shown another piece of destroyed machinery that threatens the very survival of his plant.&nbsp; Stark changes to Iron Man to help clean up the damage, but is soon observed by the Melter, who has remained nearby to survey the mess he's made of the factory, happy that he's got Stark's company on the ropes!&nbsp; But the arrival of Iron Man to help clear away the rubble causes Horgan to head back to the plant, vowing to stop him at all costs.\n\nThe factory's alarm is soon sounded -- the Melter has been spotted!&nbsp; Everyone should run for cover!\n\nEveryone but Happy and Pepper does (they race <i>toward</i> the Melter, determined to help in any way possible), and finally, they arrive just as the Melter and Iron Man come face-to-face for the final \"showdown.\"\n\nBut when the Melter tries to attack Iron Man again, his armor doesn't melt!&nbsp; And when Iron Man advances on him, the villain causes a distraction by destroying a crane carrying heavy rubble, and the huge debris threatens to crush Happy and Pepper, forcing Iron Man to save them.&nbsp; This gives the Melter time to escape by melting through the floor and diving down into the \"underground disposal system.\"&nbsp; There, he is washed away, leaving Iron Man to wonder if he drowned.\n\nThen Iron Man turns to face the reader and explains that the Melter wasn't really facing Iron Man, since he redesigned his armor, \"making it entirely out of tough extruded aluminum!\"\n\nThe crisis now over, Stark returns to the plant to order everyone back to work!",
  "grade_one_liner": "The villain is forgettable, but the pacing, tension, and industrial flavor make the story quietly satisfying.&nbsp; You close it thinking, 'That was better than I expected.'",
  "subtitle": "The Story That Melted Away My Trepidation",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | MelterIsBorn  | width=14%] A villain named the \"Melter\" seems right on brand for a character like Iron Man, and in the opening pages, we're introduced to Bruno Horgan, whose company was discovered using inferior materials as part of its government contracts, ultimately awarding them to <i>Stark's</i> company rather than Horgan's.&nbsp; It's a clean, self‑inflicted downfall -- no sabotage, no conspiracy, just Horgan's own shortcuts catching up to him.\n[BR]\n[BR] And then, on page 3, the flashback itself reveals that the very device that allowed him to dissolve iron accidentally came about because of the \"inferior parts\" in his laboratory.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Any reader with an <i>ounce</i> of sense could see the handwriting on the wall...]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Can you spell \"foreshadowing?\"&nbsp; It's not subtle, but early‑Marvel rarely is -- and that's part of the charm.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | MeltedArm | width=15%] When Iron Man first confronts the Melter inside the factory's power plant, the Melter comments that Iron Man must work for Stark.&nbsp; This may be the first time such an arrangement has been suggested.&nbsp; As recently as two issues ago ([LINK:1963:TOS_45_iron0|TOS #45|2026-11-07]), Stark hired Happy Hogan to be his \"chauffeur/bodyguard,\" and page 8 of that story only says that Iron Man is a \"good friend\" of Stark's who \"always drops in when he's in the neighborhood.\"&nbsp; That doesn't sound very formal.\n[BR]\n[BR] Later in that issue, Jack Frost, that month's villain, even says, \"<i>Iron Man! </i>I didn't expect <i>you</i> to be here!\"&nbsp; And the story concludes with Happy realizing that \"Stark needs a heap of protecting... and <i>Iron Man</i> may not always be here!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] But now it seems that people are beginning to assume that Iron Man must actually be <i>employed</i> by Stark, which is a reasonable-enough assumption, given his constant presence.\n\nThe actual working arrangement aside, it seems clear that the Melter's weapon must <i>not </i>be heat-based, or else Tony Stark's left arm would have been exposed to [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=molten iron and scalded].&nbsp; But because the story never hints that he feels any discomfort at <i>all</i>, readers are left to assume that the metal simply <b><i>dissolved</i></b> rather than <b><i>melted</i></b>. This tracks with page 3's flashback scene, where Horgan himself actually uses the term \"dissolve.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=That single word does a lot of heavy lifting, because it preserves the internal logic of Iron Man's survival without requiring any tortured explanations.]\n\nIron Man very sensibly turns and flees the scene, not because he's actually <i>afraid</i> of the Melter, but because of how this particular opponent poses a unique threat to his life-giving armor <i>and</i> how it protects his injured heart.&nbsp; It's one of the rare moments where the strip reminds readers that Iron Man isn't just armored -- he's <i>fragile</i>.&nbsp; The armor may be imposing, but the man inside is still [SPAN: severity=error; text=one malfunction away from disaster!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=Say what you want about the Melter, but this is a <b>powerful motivating factor</b> on Iron Man's part.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | FastTravel | width=50%] This leads to a brief withdrawal from the action, and just because I happened to notice it...\n[BR]\n[BR] Page 10 shows that it only takes Iron Man \"minutes\" to fly from Flushing, Long Island to the outskirts of Washington DC.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=That's some <i>seriously </i>fast travel time, even by early-Marvel standards!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nUltimately, the two face each other again, and this time (quite <i>inexplicably</i>), the Melter's weapon proves ineffective against Iron Man.\n[ASSET: center | NotMelting | width=67%]\n\nAnd once the Melter realizes he's not able to affect Iron Man's armor anymore, he escapes by diving down through a hole he made in the floor.&nbsp; And so I think it's only fair to ask:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Was the floor made <i>entirely</i> of iron and steel?&nbsp; <i>Was there no wood or concrete present at all?</i>]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Shrugs...)]&nbsp; It's one of those moments where the plot needs a hole, so the floor obligingly becomes meltable.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Naturally!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | CaryBates | width=13%] The final page's cheesy, Cary Bates-<i>Flash</i>-like address to the reader made me laugh briefly.&nbsp; I actually was a fan of that era's Flash and always got a kick out of how everyone would talk directly to the reader.&nbsp; (And for the record, even though it <i>looks</i> like he's breaking the 4<sup>th</sup> wall, in <i>actuality</i>, he's only <i>thinking</i> to himself, so it's not exactly the same thing.&nbsp; <i>Still... i</i>t's playful and gives the issue a little personality bump right before the curtain falls.)\n\nAnd the story actually <i>surprised me a bit</i> in that [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=I was wrong] about my suspicion that the inferior parts might play a role in the Melter's downfall.&nbsp; <b>They did not</b>, so [SPAN: severity=success; text=good job there] - <i>you fooled me</i>!\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | HappyAndPepper | width=15%] Happy and Pepper seem to be in every other panel in this story, as if forcefully squirted into the plot merely to provide a way to avoid additional narration.&nbsp; It seemed a bit much to me that they happened to be witnesses to <i>everything</i> that was going on.&nbsp; They're even on the scene during the final confrontation, forcing Iron Man to briefly pause to save them from a melted crane.&nbsp; It's very much in keeping with the era's habit of keeping supporting characters in motion, even when the plot doesn't strictly need them.\n\nAs for the artwork, both the cover and splash page say that this is a Lee, Ditko and Heck classic, but a mere glance at the artwork suggests that it was drawn by Heck and <i>not</i> Ditko, so perhaps Ditko did only layouts?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=(The credits are <i>unclear</i>,] but in fairness, this era often seems to feature artists tag-team pages in ways that aren't necessarily reflected in the credits.)\n\nIf Ditko did indeed do the pencils and Heck the inking, there's <i>absolutely</i> no evidence of it.&nbsp; Ditko's styling of blocking characters, splayed-out fingers, and facial close-ups are not present at <i>all</i> -- this seemed all Heck to me!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Yet the Special Announcement in this month's FF issue 20 actually <i>says</i> that Heck was the inker, so that leaves Ditko as the penciler.)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=What the... er... Heck?]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'll say this for the tale -- it was a lot better than anything we've seen come out of this magazine in recent months.&nbsp; I found myself wondering how Iron Man would defeat the Melter and had no idea why his armor didn't melt in the final pages, so again... [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=great job by the creators!]\n\nIt's brisk, coherent, and actually builds tension -- three things this feature hasn't always managed at the same time.&nbsp; It feels like the creative team finally found a rhythm that suits the character.\n\nOther than the debut issue, this was the first Iron Man story that never caused me to lose interest.&nbsp; <i>Sure</i>, the fact that Iron Man's armor \"melted\" without causing him any obvious discomfort was a [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"Wait...<i>What?</i>\"] moment, but one needed only flip back a couple of pages to see that the \"melting\" was actually \"<b>dissolving</b>,\" and so the problem was easily explained away (even though it was never directly addressed in the actual story pages).&nbsp; It also reinforces the idea that the Melter's threat is technological rather than elemental, which makes him a better fit for an Iron Man story.\n\nAnd the only other moment where I did a double-take was the quick flight that Iron Man made from Long Island to Washington DC (<i>and that was a minor plot point</i>).\n\nOutside of these, [SPAN: severity=success; text=the story was engaging and made good use of its unique aspects.]&nbsp; Stark owns a munitions factory, something true of no one else in Marvel's cast of characters thus far, and this gives the strip a grounded, industrial flavor that sets it apart from the more fantastical titles.\n\nThe story also devoted pages to the relationship that Stark International has with the US government and used Happy and Pepper throughout its pages as surrogate narrators.&nbsp; Perhaps they <i>were</i> a little too involved and indeed, seemed to be <i>everywhere</i>, but it wasn't so much as to be distracting.\n\nThe one minor bit of world-building present here -- that Iron Man <i>works for Stark</i> -- was a [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=nice addition] and deserves to be recognized as well.&nbsp; It subtly shifts Iron Man from lone adventurer to corporate defender, which opens the door to more stories rooted in industry and sabotage, a role that fits naturally with Stark's expanding industrial footprint.\n\nAnd what I thought was <b>blatant foreshadowing</b> near the beginning of the tale turned out to be a false alarm, so the writer pulled the wool over my eyes!&nbsp; (I thought I had solved the case before the final scene, and I was mistaken.&nbsp; It's a nice reminder that even in these early days, the writers occasionally zig when you expect them to zag.)\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It was a balanced and competent entry, though obviously not a perfect one.]\n\nThe Melter may never turn out to be a top-tier antagonist for Iron Man, but the Marvel staff used what they had <i>well</i> and provided a decent tale this month that I thought brought enough value to be considered mildly good.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=I enjoyed the read.]\n\nIt's not a landmark issue, but it's a confident one -- and that goes a long way in these early days of Iron Man's run.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] I pointed out in my review of [LINK:1963:TOS_45_iron0|TOS #45|2026-11-07] that the story paid no lip service to the loud [SOUNDEFFECT: text=CLANK! CLANK!] sound effects of Iron Man's metal boots in one of the factory's hidden corridors.&nbsp; This issue goes out of its way on page 5 to point out, \"Good thing I made this tunnel <i>sound-proof</i>!&nbsp; The thumping of my iron boots would be a dead giveaway if anyone should hear them!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Heh</i>, you can only wonder if the magazine hadn't received some kind of fan mail demanding an answer to this very matter...\n\n[HANG0] This issue's backup story \"Shock!\" elicited a belly laugh, even though it was <i>painfully</i> obvious early on how it would end.&nbsp; It was still good enough to earn a place as a finalist for this year's Best Backup Story Award.&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha</i>!"
  ],
  "ownership": "As for me, I got a VG/FN copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid about $21, selling it on eBay in March 2021 for $134, netting me over $95 -- <i>nice</i>!",
  "highlight": "Iron Man squares off against the Melter in a tale that dissolves more doubts than armor."
}